{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 3 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 3 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_5>\ns = input()\n\n# Replace first occurrence of 'at' with '@'\nfor i in range(len(s) - 1):\n    if s[i:i+2] == 'at':\n        s = s[:i] + '@' + s[i+2:]\n        break\n\n# Handle 'dot' replacements if any exist\nif 'dot' in s:\n    s = s.replace('dot', '.')\n    \n    # Replace first character if it's a dot\n    if s[0] == '.':\n        s = 'dot' + s[1:]\n    \n    # Replace last character if it's a dot\n    if len(s) > 0 and s[-1] == '.':\n        s = s[:-1] + 'dot'\n\nprint(s)\n</your_rewritten_code_5>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\ns = input()\n\n# First, replace all 'dot' with '.' temporarily to identify structure\ntemp = s.replace('dot', '.')\n\n# Find the position of '@' (replace first 'at' with '@')\nat_pos = -1\nfor i in range(len(temp) - 1):\n    if temp[i:i+2] == 'at':\n        at_pos = i\n        break\n\n# If no '@' found, we need to find where to place it\nif at_pos == -1:\n    # Try to find a reasonable split point - after first dot or middle\n    dot_pos = temp.find('.')\n    if dot_pos != -1 and dot_pos > 0 and dot_pos < len(temp) - 1:\n        # Split at the dot position\n        at_pos = dot_pos\n    else:\n        # Default to middle position\n        at_pos = len(temp) // 2\n\n# Build the email address with exactly one '@'\n# We need to choose where to place '@' and handle dots properly\n# The idea: find all possible positions for '@' and choose the best one\n\n# First, let's try to reconstruct by finding all 'at' occurrences and 'dot' occurrences\n# We'll consider replacing 'at' with '@' only once, and 'dot' with '.'\n\n# Better approach: identify all potential '@' positions (where 'at' occurs)\nat_positions = []\nfor i in range(len(s) - 1):\n    if s[i:i+2] == 'at':\n        at_positions.append(i)\n\n# If no 'at' found, we need to insert '@' at a valid position\nif not at_positions:\n    # Try to find a valid position for '@' that makes a proper email\n    # Must not be at start or end, and must allow for valid structure\n    # We'll try positions after first letter and before last letter\n    candidates = []\n    for pos in range(1, len(s)):\n        # Build candidate email\n        candidate = s[:pos] + '@' + s[pos:]\n        # Replace all 'dot' with '.'\n        candidate = candidate.replace('dot', '.')\n        # Check if valid\n        if candidate.count('@') == 1 and candidate[0] != '.' and candidate[-1] != '.' and candidate[0] != '@' and candidate[-1] != '@':\n            # Check all characters are valid\n            valid = True\n            for c in candidate:\n                if c not in '.@' and (c < 'a' or c > 'z'):\n                    valid = False\n                    break\n            if valid:\n                candidates.append(candidate)\n    \n    # If no candidates found with manual '@' insertion, try other approaches\n    if not candidates:\n        # Try replacing 'dot' first, then find where to put '@'\n        temp = s.replace('dot', '.')\n        # Try each position where we could have had 'at'\n        for i in range(1, len(temp)):\n            candidate = temp[:i] + '@' + temp[i:]\n            if candidate.count('@') == 1 and candidate[0] != '.' and candidate[-1] != '.' and candidate[0] != '@' and candidate[-1] != '@':\n                valid = True\n                for c in candidate:\n                    if c not in '.@' and (c < 'a' or c > 'z'):\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if valid:\n                    candidates.append(candidate)\n    \n    # Choose the shortest, then lexicographically smallest\n    if candidates:\n        candidates.sort(key=lambda x: (len(x), x))\n        print(candidates[0])\n    else:\n        # Fallback: just replace 'dot' with '.' and try to fix start/end\n        result = s.replace('dot', '.')\n        if result[0] == '.':\n            result = 'a' + result\n        if result[-1] == '.':\n            result = result[:-1] + 'a'\n        if result[0] == '@' or result[-1] == '@':\n            result = 'a' + result.strip('@')\n        print(result)\nelse:\n    # We have 'at' positions, replace the first one with '@'\n    s = s[:at_positions[0]] + '@' + s[at_positions[0]+2:]\n    # Replace all 'dot' with '.'\n    s = s.replace('dot', '.')\n    \n    # Fix if starts or ends with dot or @\n    while s and (s[0] == '.' or s[0] == '@'):\n        s = s[1:]\n    while s and (s[-1] == '.' or s[-1] == '@'):\n        s = s[:-1]\n    \n    print(s)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.3\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-5.960462772236497e-07, -0.0003051292151212692, -1.4305104514278355e-06, -1.1920922133867862e-06, -0.03793777897953987, -8.344646857949556e-07, -1.7881377516459906e-06, -0.002372785471379757, -7.152531907195225e-06, -0.513042688369751, -0.0013410394312813878, -0.35640281438827515, -0.03452647104859352, -0.4786086678504944, -0.1270202398300171, -0.0009176096064038575, -0.447822630405426, -0.4877892732620239, -0.19014157354831696, -0.11060626804828644, -0.6507200598716736, -0.7181814312934875, -0.00010561384988250211, -0.3756564259529114, -0.0007185738068073988, -5.960462772236497e-07, -0.002809032564982772, -5.960462772236497e-07, -0.0028293600771576166, -5.602820692729438e-06, -0.0016091029392555356, -0.00025912740966305137, -2.3841830625315197e-06, -0.5248583555221558, -0.4729141592979431, -0.11292701959609985, -4.60137271147687e-05, -0.00044860312482342124, -0.01612279750406742, -0.7098393440246582, -0.9180328249931335, -0.38799288868904114, -0.007623862475156784, -4.768370445162873e-07, -0.02634967863559723, -0.11927907913923264, -0.0008561521535739303, -0.027238331735134125, -0.06215628236532211, -0.005933172535151243, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -0.004152723588049412, 0.0, -2.3841855067985307e-07, -0.006211381405591965, 0.0, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -1.1920928244535389e-07, -9.536738616588991e-07, -0.0036042272113263607, -1.2040065485052764e-05, 0.0, 0.0, -4.76837